  "live_records": {
    "q-330842": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330842",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
