    "q-330844": {
      "dom_id": "step-q330844",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{120}{{s}^{7}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{120}{{s}^{7}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{120}{{s}^{6}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{120}{{s}^{6}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{5!}{{s}^{5+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{5}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
